==Production==
Following the financial success of the revival of the Frankenstein series with ''[[Son of Frankenstein]]'' and The Invisible Man series with ''[[The Invisible Man Returns]]'', [[Universal Pictures]] decided to revive their [[The Mummy (Universal film franchise)|''The Mummy'' series]] with ''The Mummy's Hand''.{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=229}} The film's budget was set at $80,000 and began filming towards the end of May 1940.{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=229}}

The authors of the book ''Universal Horrors'' described the budget as "modest" and noted that cost-cutting for the film involved using stock shots taken from ''[[The Mummy (1932 film)|The Mummy]]'', leftover sets from [[James Whale]]'s film ''[[Green Hell (film)|Green Hell]]'', and musical scores almost entirely lifted from ''Son of Frankenstein''.{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=229}}{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=230}} The producer for the film was Ben Pivar, who [[Reginald LeBorg]] described as the epitome artless, noncreative studio executive who was often crude and occasionally seemed illiterate.{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=230}}

''The Mummy's Hand''{{'}}s production continued into mid-June, which led director [[Christy Cabanne]] and his crew to film into overtime hours.{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=230}}{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=231}} According to [[Peggy Moran]], she had to be on set at 6am to do hair and makeup and filming began at 8am and would occasionally work as late as 4am the next day.  
{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=231}} Moran commented that "they could do that with people like me because we were under contract. The law requires that outside talent only work for X-number of hours, but me they could work all the time!" {{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=231}} When asked about her fellow cast, she spoke positively about Dick Foran ("very nice and friendly") and Wallace Ford ("very funny always").{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=233}} On [[Tom Tyler]] who played [[Kharis]], she never met him without his make-up on, stating that he had to be at the studio at four in the morning and he couldn't talk with his make-up on. Dick Foran, who played main character Steve Banning, was an established star when he made this, but his career prior to ''The Mummy's Hand'' consisted mostly of supporting roles in second tier Universal films and leading roles in "[[singing cowboy]]" movies. {{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=235}}

The film went over budget and slightly over schedule, costing an extra $4,000. Editing of the film was finished before the end of June.{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=231}}

==Release==
''The Mummy's Hand'' was distributed by [[Universal Pictures|Universal Pictures Co.]] on September 20, 1940.<ref name="afi">{{cite web|url=https://catalog.afi.com/Film/8151-THE-MUMMYSHAND?sid=798b2519-1f28-4438-811f-3d06bb2208b4&sr=9.412861&cp=1&pos=0|publisher=[[American Film Institute]]|title=The Mummy's Hand (1940)|access-date=June 1, 2020|archive-date=July 18, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220718133330/https://catalog.afi.com/Film/8151-THE-MUMMYSHAND?sid=798b2519-1f28-4438-811f-3d06bb2208b4&sr=9.412861&cp=1&pos=0|url-status=live}}</ref> According to ''[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]'', their screening of the film had certain scenes tinted an "eerie green".{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=231}}

Unlike some of the other horror films produced by Universal, ''The Mummy's Hand'' was not reissued for theatrical release in the late 1940s and early 1950s.{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=237}} ''The Mummy's Hand'' was followed by the sequel ''[[The Mummy's Tomb]]'', released in 1942. ''The Mummy's Tomb'' takes place 30 years after the events of ''The Mummy's Hand'', with Foran, Wallace, and Zucco reprising their respective roles as Steve Banning, Babe Hanson, and Andoheb. The film re-uses several minutes of footage from ''The Mummy's Hand'' in the form of flashback sequences.{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=237}}{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=305}}

===Home media===
In the 1990s, [[MCA/Universal Home Video]] released ''The Mummy's Hand'' on [[VHS]] as part of the "Universal Monsters Classic Collection", a series of releases of [[Universal Classic Monsters]] films. In 1994, MCA/Universal released the film on [[LaserDisc]] as part of ''The Mummy Collection'' six-disc set, which also includes ''[[The Mummy's Tomb]]'' (1942), ''[[The Mummy's Ghost]]'', and ''[[The Mummy's Curse]]'' (both 1944).<ref>{{cite magazine|date=January 15, 1994|title=Laser Scans|last=McGowan|first=Chris|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=FwgEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PA51|magazine=[[Billboard (magazine)|Billboard]]|volume=106|issue=3|page=51|issn=0006-2510|access-date=September 19, 2023}}</ref>

On October 19, 2004, Universal released ''The Mummy's Hand''—along with ''The Mummy'', ''The Mummy's Tomb'', ''The Mummy's Ghost'', and ''The Mummy's Curse''—on [[DVD]] as part of the two-disc set ''The Mummy: The Legacy Collection''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dvdtalk.com/dvdsavant/s1464mumm.html|title=DVD Savant Review: The Mummy: The Legacy Collection|last=Erickson|first=Glenn|date=October 19, 2004|website=[[DVD Talk]]|access-date=September 19, 2023}}</ref> In 2014, Universal re-released the five films on DVD as a set titled ''The Mummy: Complete Legacy Collection''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amazon.com/Mummy-Complete-Legacy-Collection/dp/B00L8QP0U0/|title=The Mummy: Complete Legacy Collection [DVD]|website=[[Amazon.com]]|date=2 September 2014|access-date=September 19, 2023|archive-date=August 12, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240812182010/https://www.amazon.com/Mummy-Complete-Legacy-Collection/dp/B00L8QP0U0|url-status=live}}</ref> In 2017, ''The Mummy: Complete Legacy Collection'' was released on [[Blu-ray]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amazon.com/Mummy-Complete-Legacy-Collection-Blu-ray/dp/B06VYGFK9M/|title=The Mummy: Complete Legacy Collection [Blu-ray]|website=[[Amazon.com]]|date=16 May 2017|access-date=September 19, 2023|archive-date=January 21, 2024|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240121021343/https://www.amazon.com/Mummy-Complete-Legacy-Collection-Blu-ray/dp/B06VYGFK9M|url-status=live}}</ref>

''The Mummy's Hand'' was included in the ''Universal Classic Monsters: Complete 30-Film Collection'' Blu-ray box set in August 2018.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amazon.com/Universal-Classic-Monsters-Complete-Collection/dp/B07DKY35N5/|title=Universal Classic Monsters: Complete 30-Film Collection [Blu-ray]|website=[[Amazon.com]]|date=28 August 2018 |access-date=September 19, 2023|archive-date=January 21, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200121043921/https://www.amazon.com/Universal-Classic-Monsters-Complete-Collection/dp/B07DKY35N5/|url-status=live}}</ref> This box set was also issued in the DVD format.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.amazon.com/Classic-Monsters-Complete-30-Film-Collection/dp/B00L8QP082/|title=Classic Monsters (Complete 30-Film Collection) [DVD]|website=[[Amazon.com]]|date=2 September 2014 |access-date=September 19, 2023|archive-date=January 21, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200121044256/https://www.amazon.com/Classic-Monsters-Complete-30-Film-Collection/dp/B00L8QP082/|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Critical reception==
From contemporary reviews, film critic [[Bosley Crowther]] of ''[[The New York Times]]'' commented on the film's use of trope and cliché, and added, "frightening or funny, take your choice".{{sfn|Crowther|1940}} The ''[[Philadelphia Record]]'' found that the film's plot was "sheer nonsense" but that the film "manages to raise a few more goose pimples than other recent horror movies".{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=237}} "Hobe" of ''[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]'' found the film to be "muddled in the writing and clumsy in the production. Direction and photography are bush league. Acting varies from violent mugging to smooth under-playing".{{sfn|Weaver|Brunas|Brunas|2007|p=237}}

[[James Lowder]] reviewed ''The Mummy's Hand'' in ''[[White Wolf Inphobia]]'' #53 (March, 1995), rating it a 3 1/2 out of 5 and stated that "The mummy's defeat (actually, its apparent defeat, since it returns for ''The Mummy's Tomb'') is one of the most pathetic endings for a monster film in history – set ablaze as it lies face-down on the floor, lapping up spilled tana tea. Very unsportsmanlike of Banning, but Kharis evens the score in the next entry."<ref name="WW53">{{Cite magazine |last=Lowder |first=James |authorlink=James Lowder |date=March 1995|title=Into the Dark |magazine= [[White Wolf Inphobia]] |number=53 |pages=57–58 |url=https://imgur.com/a/bHo2OkF}}</ref>

Graeme Clark of ''The Spinning Image'', comparing the film with Boris Karloff's and granting 6 out of 10 stars, writes: "This was no eerie love story across the millennia, this was straight fright fare with Universal Studios' least-loved monster, here in the form that viewers would know him best, shambling, strangling, singleminded and mute." He also appreciated some of the special effects.{{sfn|Clark}}

On the [[review aggregator]] website [[Rotten Tomatoes]], the film holds a 60% approval rating based on 10 reviews, with an average score of 5.8/10.<ref>{{cite web |date= |title=The Mummy's Hand |url=http://www.rottentomatoes.com/m/mummys_hand/ |access-date=October 24, 2023 |publisher=Rotten Tomatoes}}</ref>

